{
  "slug": "find-pivot-index",
  "title": "Find Pivot Index",
  "topic": "arrays-hashing",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "pattern": "prefix sum / running total",
  "statement": "Given an array of integers `nums`, an index `i` is a pivot index when the sum of all elements strictly to the left of `i` equals the sum of all elements strictly to the right of `i`. A sum over an empty range counts as `0`, so index `0` is a pivot index whenever everything after it sums to zero, and the last index qualifies whenever everything before it sums to zero.\n\nReturn the leftmost pivot index of `nums`, or `-1` when no index satisfies the condition.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums.length <= 200` and `-10000 <= nums[i] <= 10000`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums = [-10000]",
      "output": "0",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [-221, 6635, 9954, -3483]",
      "output": "-1",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Knowing the total sum, can you express the sum to the right of index i using only the sum to its left and the element at i?",
    "Sweep once from left to right, keeping the sum of everything strictly before the current index in a single variable.",
    "The right side is `total - left - nums[i]`; compare first, then fold the current element into the left sum."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force rebuilds both side sums from scratch for every candidate index using two inner loops, which is O(n\u00b2) total comparisons. The optimal version pays one O(n) pass to compute the total sum, then sweeps left to right while holding the running sum of the elements strictly before the current index. The right-hand sum is then `total - left - nums[i]`, computable in constant time, so the first index that satisfies the equality is the leftmost pivot index and the scan can return immediately without sorting or a second pass.",
    "why_optimal": "A valid answer can appear anywhere, so every index must be examined once, giving an \u03a9(n) lower bound; the prefix sweep inspects each index exactly once with constant work and only two scalar variables, meeting that bound with O(1) extra space at the `nums.length <= 200` limit.",
    "pitfalls": "The empty-side convention is the most common source of wrong answers: index 0 is valid whenever the suffix sums to zero, which includes the all-zero array where the expected answer is 0. A frequent bug seeds `left` with `nums[0]` and then tests the equality at the same index, shifting every answer by one; another computes the right side as `total - left` and forgets to subtract the pivot element itself. Update the running left sum after the comparison, return `-1` only once the loop has finished, and note that Python slicing in the brute force allocates new lists while the JavaScript brute force stays in place."
  },
